# Data licence — read this before reusing anything in derived/

**Three different regimes in one repository. There is no single licence for
`derived/`, and there never was.**

## What the 22 sources say

| sources | what the registry declares | can this repo offer CC-BY-4.0? |
| ---: | --- | --- |
| 19 | US federal work, no copyright in the US (FDA) | **No** — public domain; there is no right to license |
| 1 | `tga.shortages.au` — Australian Government (TGA), site content CC BY 4.0 unless marked | Yes, passed through, attribution to TGA |
| 1 | `ansm.shortages.fr` — ANSM public information | **No** — a description, not a grant |
| 1 | `ie.hpra.products` — unstated on hpra.ie | **No** — explicitly unstated |

The previous version of this file applied the verbatim CC-BY-4.0 text to all
22 at once. That is wrong three separate ways: it claims a right that does not
exist over the FDA rows, invents one over ANSM and HPRA, and is redundant for
the single source that genuinely grants it.

Note the TGA qualifier the registry preserved — *unless marked*. Individual
pages can carry their own terms, so the grant is a default, not a guarantee.

## What this repository grants

| what | licence |
| --- | --- |
| the code | MIT, see [LICENSE](LICENSE) |
| FDA-derived rows | public domain upstream; no onward licence claimed |
| TGA-derived rows | CC BY 4.0, passed through; attribute the Commonwealth of Australia |
| ANSM- and HPRA-derived rows | no grant upstream; no onward licence offered |

Rows carry `source_id`, so which regime applies to a given row is determinable
from the data rather than from this file.

Checked 22 September 2026 against the `licence:` field of all 22 registry files.
